# TODO

1. A force switch, so that an existing CHANGELOG can be overwritten rather than the directory being skipped.
2. Take the binary's name from the CHANGELOG, rather than assuming the sole entry in bin/.
3. Produce a single CHANGELOG in the last version directory, rather than a cumulative one in every directory.
4. Work for git repositories, whether by writing outside the repository or by rewriting history.
5. A tier for a snapshot: a revision whose header carries no new version and no new Changes item. Such a revision is a working copy taken between versions, not a version, and today its entry is summarised by the first line of its diff, so a revision in which a fixture was regenerated under the next name and the test script changed reads as `- test/6.0.csv`. SimpleCSV's staging tree has seven (`0.2.2` once, `0.4.6` four times, `0.4.9` once, and a second `0.9.3`). Summarise such a revision by the whole diff, and give it a housekeeping subject with no version at all, `~ test/test.rb, test/6.0.csv --> test/6.1.csv`, since a commit titled with a version is a version, one each (decided 20260909).
6. Take the entry's date from the latest header in the tree, not only the main file's. SimpleCSV's second `0.9.3` changed only `String/split_csv.rb`, whose header says 20100612, and was dated 20100526 from the library header. When these two are done, the imported histories can have their narration regenerated in one pass: `dsv` needs a re-import through `_github_staging/SimpleCSV/synthetic/` and a force-push, so it is done once, before any consumer points at the repository.
7. Multiple dates in a CHANGELOG heading, as a header's date line already carries them: `## 20260913, 1005`, escalating against the element before it, the last being the completion date.  Both tools want it and one fails quietly.  Here, `entries_from`'s heading pattern is `## (\S+)`, which cannot span the space after the comma, so such a heading is not recognised at all and its entry takes whatever date was last seen; `expand_dates` already reads the escalating form for a header's date line and would serve; and `write_changelog` writes the single `entry[:date]` it holds, so it would have to keep the heading it read rather than reduce it.  In git-import-all, `DatedHeading::HEADING` and `BOUNDARY` want eight digits exactly, so it raises `No CHANGELOG entries recognised`, and the entry should take the last of the dates.  Nothing needs this: changelogger writes a single date by design, reducing a multi-date header to its completion date, so only a hand-written CHANGELOG would carry one.  Raised upon dsv 0.12.4, whose work spanned 20260913 and 1005; the span went into the entry's prose instead, which both parsers pass through untouched.
